# LumOn Surface Cache Lighting Integration and Test Coverage

## 1. Dynamic lighting history
- [x] Add a reusable controlled-scene test for removing and restoring light after the surface cache has accumulated illumination.
- [x] Verify production history invalidation removes stale illumination, unresolved geometry adds no samples, and one complete page update produces the new lighting; unchanged inputs retain accumulation.
- [x] Extend the fixture with doorway closure/reopening and verify stale illumination disappears within a defined update budget.

Verified by `SurfaceCacheDynamicLightingTests.LightRemovalAndRestorationDiscardAccumulatedHistory`: four initial bright samples, light off/on, all 64 texels checked for RGB and sample weight. Each settled transition uses one page update with one ray per texel and a 256-step traversal limit. This exercises shared geometry publication and the production history-reset owner, not the complete renderer lifecycle or its page requeue orchestration.

`SurfaceCacheDynamicLightingTests.DoorwayClosureAndReopeningInvalidateIllumination` covers a dark enclosed room with fixed bright exterior lighting. Only the doorway occupancy changes; GPU readback verifies the door changes and the captured back wall remains unchanged. After four accumulated open-door samples, closing the door clears history and produces valid dark samples across all 64 texels. Reopening restores the original attenuated RGB pattern. Unpublished geometry must fail relighting without adding sample weight. Each transition settles within one complete page update after publication, using one ray per texel and a 256-step traversal limit.

Validation: normal SPIR-V-enabled build passed; both dynamic-history cases and existing shared-surface tests passed 13/13 with no skips. Receipts: `artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-cache-doorway-finalv2-build.log` and `surface-cache-doorway-finalv2.trx` in the same directory.

## 2. Full runtime orchestration
- [x] Exercise the production surface-cache renderer lifecycle: initialization, feedback, allocation, capture, relighting, debug-view activation, and disposal.
- [x] Verify published resources and rendered output across multiple frames, including resource recreation, without OpenGL errors.

Verified by `SurfaceCacheRuntimeTests.RegisteredCallbacksDriveSurfaceCacheAcrossRecreationAndWorldRestart`, which drives the registered production callbacks through controlled engine boundaries. The reusable fixture publishes controlled geometry before submitting feedback, then verifies allocation, successful capture and relight completion, debug-view callback execution, physical-atlas recreation, world teardown and restart, and callback cleanup. `DisposedRenderersRemoveTheirEngineCallbacks` independently guards renderer unregistration, while `RuntimePublishedGeometrySupportsSurfaceCapture` correlates runtime-published geometry with both the established source-compiled capture fixture and the production SPIR-V capture owner.

The runtime fixture intentionally treats nonzero final lighting as separate coverage. This section proves that a resident page becomes sampleable and survives the required lifecycle transitions; Section 7 owns proof that cached lighting reaches final indirect-lighting pixels.

Validation: normal incremental build passed; `SurfaceCacheRuntimeTests` passed 3/3 with no skips. Isolated neighboring runs also passed: `SurfaceCacheDynamicLightingTests` 2/2 and `SharedTraceSceneSurfaceTests` 11/11. Receipts: `artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-cache-runtime-current-v40-tests.log`, `surface-cache-dynamic-isolated-tests.log`, and `surface-cache-shared-surface-isolated-tests.log`, with their corresponding `.trx` files.

## 3. Surface-cache consumer audit

Status: historical consumer audit complete; hit-consumer implementation is tracked in Section 6 and full behavioral verification remains Section 7.

- [x] Identify the currently implemented surface-cache consumers and explicitly record any missing integration before defining end-to-end expectations.

The original audit found only a diagnostic surface-cache consumer. Production screen/world-probe hit lighting had no connection to the cache. Section 6 now implements that connection and replaces the missing-integration assertion with positive hit-consumer coverage. Gather and final composition remain downstream of probe radiance and do not bind the surface cache directly.

The illumination/removal proof across filtering, gather and final pixels remains Section 7. The validation below records the original audit, not that later end-to-end behavior.
Validation: the focused consumer-contract run passed 2/2 with no failures or skips and built all 98 shader stages / 243 SPIR-V variants. Receipts: `artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-cache-consumer-contract-tests.log` and `surface-cache-consumer-contract.trx`.

Use modular fixtures shared across scenarios. Record executed tests and results when completing each item.

## 4. Surface lighting terms and storage contract

Controlling design and task traceability: [Surface-cache lighting contract](LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md). Its storage design governs Sections 5–7; Section 4 implements the estimator corrections without claiming the later producers or consumers exist.

- [x] Define separate direct irradiance, indirect irradiance, linear diffuse albedo, and emitted radiance terms. Specify units, exposure convention, sky/block-light classification, and normalization for every producer and consumer.
- [x] Define cached outgoing diffuse radiance as `L = (E_direct + E_indirect) * diffuseAlbedo / pi + emission`, with finite, nonnegative output. Keep irradiance distinct from material-modulated outgoing radiance in names, resources, and debug views.
- [x] Audit the existing relight estimator, including angular/PDF weighting and `1 / (1 + distance^2)` attenuation; replace incompatible behavior and old test expectations with the documented lighting contract.
- [x] Specify atlas layers, per-term validity/history, and publication ownership for direct irradiance, indirect irradiance, and combined outgoing radiance. Preserve bounded allocation and update budgets.

Validation: second implementation review and independent completion audit passed. The normal shader-enabled build compiled 98 stages / 243 SPIR-V variants. Focused GPU suites passed 18/18 with zero failures or skips: lighting terms 5/5, dynamic history 2/2, shared surface 11/11. Receipts: artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-cache-terms-{lighting,dynamic,shared}.{log,trx}. New coverage checks distance invariance, nonwhite hit reflectance, rejection of incomplete hemisphere batches, and capped-history energy stability.

## 5. Produce and publish complete surface lighting

Controlling design and task traceability: [Surface-cache lighting contract](LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md).
- [x] Capture the material data needed for diffuse reflectance and emission; compute direct irradiance from supported scene lighting with the documented visibility and sky treatment.
- [x] Update indirect irradiance by tracing against previously published surface outgoing radiance, with correct integration weights and bounded temporal accumulation. Prevent same-dispatch read/write feedback and double-counting direct light or emission.
- [x] Combine the lighting terms into outgoing radiance and publish coherent page mappings, generations, readiness, and lighting resources through a read-only provider.
- [x] Invalidate affected lighting and dependent indirect history when lights, materials, geometry, residency, or atlas resources change; verify bounded convergence after light removal and restoration.
- [x] Add reusable numerical tests for isolated direct, indirect, and emissive terms, albedo scaling, normalization, valid darkness, and stable multi-bounce updates.

Validation: second implementation review and independent completion audit passed. Normal shader-enabled build compiled 99 stages / 244 variants. Focused suites passed 44/44 with zero failures or skips: producers 7, runtime 5, pool/budget units 10, dynamic history 2, shared surface 11, estimator terms 5, debug bindings 4. Producer coverage uses real capture and previous-generation transport, checks every page completion marker and outgoing texel validity, and verifies direct/albedo/emission accounting, normalization, unavailable-page history, removal/restoration and stable multiple bounces. The runtime test verifies partial-page readiness and bitwise preservation of a previously lit tile across publication swaps, plus configuration/resource invalidation. Receipts: artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-lighting-build.log, surface-lighting-producer.trx, surface-lighting-runtime-final.{log,trx}, surface-lighting-pool.{log,trx}, and surface-lighting-{SurfaceCacheDynamicLightingTests,SharedTraceSceneSurfaceTests,SurfaceCacheLightingTermsTests,SurfaceCacheDebugBindingTests}.{log,trx}.

Provider production is complete. Section 6 owns screen/world-probe consumption; Section 7 owns final-lighting proof.

## 6. Consume surface lighting at geometry hits

Controlling design and task traceability: [Surface-cache lighting contract](LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md), especially Geometry-hit consumers and Atlas and publication design.
- [x] Implement shared hit-to-surface addressing from geometry hit position, face/material identity, and chunk generation through the page table to cached outgoing radiance. Test cross-chunk hits, boundaries, stale mappings, and unavailable pages.
- [x] Connect the published provider to `LumOnRenderer` and the screen-probe near-field hit-lighting path through shader-owned contracts. Preserve screen-hit behavior; consume cached outgoing radiance for supported world-geometry hits without applying hit albedo twice.
- [x] Define explicit unavailable-cache behavior separately from valid black lighting, and preserve hit distance, occlusion, confidence, and history semantics.
- [x] Connect world-probe trace hit lighting to the same surface-lighting contract. Resolve the current CPU-worker/GPU-cache ownership boundary explicitly; do not synchronously read GPU textures from tracing workers. Record any required GPU tracing or snapshot transport work as an implementation dependency.
- [x] Keep gather and final composition downstream of probe radiance. Reserve reflection integration for an actual reflection consumer rather than introducing an unused pipeline.

Validation: second implementation review and independent completion audit passed. Normal shader-enabled build compiled 100 stages / 245 variants. Focused suites passed 104 distinct tests with zero failures or skips: hit consumers 12, near-field behavior 57, world-probe transport 2, scheduler 5, runtime publication 5, shared surface 11, producers 7, shader bindings 3, consumer contracts 2. Coverage includes real produced radiance at screen and CPU-world-probe hits, accepted screen-hit emission exactly once, signed cross-chunk addressing, stale/aliased/unavailable pages, valid black, preserved hit distances, query-buffer reuse, dependency revisions and delayed-request rejection.

World-probe geometry tracing remains on workers. The implemented dependency is bounded asynchronous render-thread GPU hit resolution (at most 4,096 descriptors), followed by whole-probe atlas upload. Workers perform no GPU readback. Hits outside resident cache coverage remain unresolved. Final-pixel and multi-frame consumer proof remain Section 7; no live visual verification is claimed.

Receipts: artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/surface-consumer-build-confirmed.log; surface-consumer-{lookup,runtime,bindings,contract}-verified.{log,trx}; surface-consumer-{nearfield,world,scheduler}-current.{log,trx}; surface-consumer-shared-final.{log,trx}; surface-consumer-producer.{log,trx}.
## 7. End-to-end lighting proof
- [x] Replace `FinalLightingShadersDoNotYetDeclareSurfaceCacheInputs` with positive consumer coverage; absence of integration must not remain a required behavior.
- [ ] Trace controlled scene lighting changes through real cache production, hit sampling, probe radiance, filtering, both gather modes, and final indirect-lighting pixels. Verify illumination, removal, and restoration without injecting downstream lighting.
- [ ] Cover screen misses with offscreen geometry, emissive-only illumination, sealed-room darkness, multi-bounce transfer, missing/stale pages, and resource recreation. Check intermediate radiance as well as final pixels to identify the failing boundary.
- [ ] Extend world-probe coverage through its implemented cache consumer and exercise runtime publication/invalidation across frames. Record build and executed GPU-test evidence separately from live visual verification.

Implementation order: 4 → 5 → 6 → 7. Sections 4–6 own production integration; Section 7 owns the behavioral verification moved from Section 3. Missing consumer implementation must remain distinct from test coverage, and injected downstream lighting cannot substitute for cache consumption. Existing Sections 1–2 remain historical validation of the earlier estimator and lifecycle, and their numerical expectations must be updated where the lighting contract changes.
